  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant impacts will continue this afternoon \nwith cooler temperatures but dry weather on Friday and Saturday. Our \nnext chance for rain is Sunday night into early Monday.\n\nSignificant impacts have occurred today from the combination of near \nrecord or record tides, wave action, and damaging synoptic winds. In \nmany cases, coastal impacts of this magnitude have not been observed \nin over a decade. Slow improvement is expected during the next 24 \nhours as the low pressure system gradually exits the area.\n\nAfternoon wx analysis shows that the low center is close to the AKQ \noffice this afternoon with the heavy rain finally exiting the area \naside from the eastern shore. Winds have diminished substantially in \nmost areas but will remain gusty out of the NE to 50+ mph on the \neastern shore for the next few hours. The High Wind Warning remains \nin effect through 7 PM with lingering Wind Advisories farther inland \nand across the Piedmont. Winds will eventually become SW after the \noccluded front passes by this evening and will be gusty to 25-40 mph \n(highest near the coast) through Friday. Cooler with highs only in \nthe 50s on Friday. A moderating trend is expected to take place this \nweekend as high pressure moves from the Deep South to off the SE \nCONUS coast. Mild with highs in the 60s on both Saturday and Sunday. \nA fast moving low pressure system tracks just to our north Sunday \nnight while dragging a cold front through the area. Showers are \nlikely Sunday night along and ahead of the front, but it should \nmostly clear out by sunrise on Monday. Dry, breezy on Monday with \nWNW winds gusting to 20-25 mph but not much cooler (still lower 60s) \nbehind the cold front. \n \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Significant coastal flooding is expected today into \nFriday, with widespread moderate to major flooding and potentially \nrecord water levels at a few locations.\n\nSignificant coastal flooding is expected today into Friday, with \nwidespread moderate to major flooding and potentially record water \nlevels at a few locations. \n\nA significant coastal flooding event is unfolding across the region \nearly this morning as strong E-NE winds associated with deepening \nlow pressure near the NC coast combine with elevated astronomical \ntides. Coastal Flood Warnings remain in effect across the tidal \nforecast area, with widespread moderate to major flooding expected. \n\nThe latest forecast still supports potential historic flooding as \nseveral locations, especially along the Atlantic-facing Eastern \nShore and portions of the tidal James River. Forecast water levels \nat Wachapreague, Jamestown, and Lynnhaven could exceed their \nrespective historical maxima. The most significant flooding is \nexpected this morning into early afternoon at most locations. \nAdditional high tide cycles will also be impacted into Friday, \nespecially across the eastern shoreline of the Chesapeake Bay. \nWarnings have been extended into Friday night for the Maryland \nbayside counties, where major flooding will likely continue for \nmultiple cycles. Along the Atlantic coast, the warning for Ocean \nCity has also been extended into tonight to cover and additional \nhigh tide cycle. \n\nIn addition to elevated water levels, dangerous surf along the \nAtlantic coast will result in significant shoreline erosion and \nlocalized overwash. High Surf Advisories remain in effect along the \nAtlantic coast through tonight and into Friday. While confidence is \nhigh in significant coastal flooding, the exact magnitude and timing \nof the peak water levels remain sensitive to the low track, wind \ndirection, and timing of the wind shift later today.",
